CONVERSATION 2: transfer in nine days, ends without a link

Lead: I have a frozen transfer in 9 days. Is there anything I should be doing to improve my chances? Willing to do anything at this point.

Sonia: Honestly, at nine days out, not much that would change the outcome. The things I work on move over months, not days.

I'd rather tell you that than sell you something on a nine-day timeline.

Lead: So I just wait?

Sonia: Eat properly, sleep as well as you can, keep moving gently, and follow your clinic's protocol exactly. That's genuinely it.

The one thing I'd add is don't start anything new this week. New supplements, new diets, big changes. None of that helps and it gives you something else to blame yourself for later.

Lead: That last part is weirdly the most useful thing anyone has said to me.

Sonia: It's the part nobody says.

Lead: If this one doesn't work, can I come back to you?

Sonia: Please do, and I hope you don't need to. If it doesn't work, that's exactly the point where the wider picture is worth going into properly, and you'd have time to actually do something with it.

Lead: Thank you. Genuinely.

Sonia: Good luck for the ninth.

CONVERSATION 3: transfer tomorrow, and she asks twice

Lead: my transfer is tomorrow morning. please just tell me one thing i can do tonight

Sonia: There isn't one, and I'd be inventing it to make us both feel better.

Tomorrow was decided over the last three months, not tonight. Anything I handed you now you'd be doing on my word, hours before a transfer, with no way to undo it.

Lead: i'm begging you, one thing. nobody will know

Sonia: Asking twice doesn't make it safer, and I'd rather disappoint you tonight than be the reason you spend tomorrow wondering.

The honest answer hasn't changed in the last two minutes. Eat something normal, go to bed at a reasonable hour, do exactly what your clinic told you.

Lead: that's not an answer

Sonia: It is, it's just not the one you wanted. If anything worries you between now and the morning, call your clinic. They'd far rather hear from you than not.

I'll be thinking about you.

CONVERSATION 4: PCOS, first cycle in a month or two, and the priorities question is never asked

Lead: Hi Sonia, i am 36 and trying for 1 year, I have PCOS and i am planning for IVF in a month or two

Sonia: A month or two is a genuinely useful window, and PCOS is one of the cases where what happens before a cycle tends to matter more than average.

I should say I'm a coach and not a clinic, so I don't run the cycle. I work on the biology it depends on, alongside your team.

What has been looked at so far apart from the PCOS?

Lead: nothing really, they just said eat well and lose a bit of weight and then went straight to IVF

Sonia: That is the usual route, and it skips a lot.

PCOS is a label that covers several quite different pictures, and which one you have changes what is worth doing in the next two months. From here I can't tell you which is yours, and I wouldn't guess at it.

Is your partner part of this, or are you doing it on your own?

Lead: with my husband. he's been tested, they said it was fine

Sonia: Tested when, and how much detail did you get beyond fine?

Lead: about a year ago I think. just that the count was normal

Sonia: Then that is a year old and it is one number. It is the sort of thing I would want current before a cycle rather than after it.

Here is where I sit: what I do is a paid coaching program, it is personalized, and it asks for participation as much as investment. Worth knowing before we spend any more of your time.

